sections.body:
The **Operations Stage** runs from the moment the asset is built and commissioned until it is handed back to the Procuring Authority. The basic contract management mechanisms are the same as in construction, but the focus changes.

The move from construction to operations is risky. Almost all the professionals who designed, built and checked the asset leave, and **a new team** starts to operate it. The new staff often do not know why systems were designed the way they were. Assets also suffer from **performance decay**: buildings such as offices, schools and hospitals can lose as much as **30 percent of their efficiency in the first four years** of operation.

The Guide notes a weakness in many projects: the **facility manager** is rarely involved during design, although this person must later run the building. The facility manager should help weigh construction costs against operating and maintenance costs. Sometimes spending more on better construction saves money over the life of the asset.

To manage this risk, the authority should prepare a **Transition Plan**, with dependencies, timescales and resources, and update it regularly. It covers, where relevant:
- the government resources that will be in place (who, what, when);
- the move to suitable accommodation and equipment;
- a **help desk**, the contact point for users to report problems;
- contact lists and agreed lines of communication;
- a familiarisation programme so users know what services to expect;
- key milestones and a **data room** (library) of project information;
- **trial running** of the payment mechanism and performance monitoring system;
- confirmation that the facility management contractor can deliver the services.

The plan links to the communications plan. Above all, the authority must **not delay the contractor** by its own action or inaction. It needs staff and systems ready, and a full-time, respected senior person on site through the transition.
